From df26942dca7e1c07d5cc788c1a9c679a1c3a9a7e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: L0garithmic <L0garithmic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 14:14:43 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: implement IDBIndex queries, and fix an index
 cursor losing its position

IDBIndex had no methods at all: get, getKey, getAll, getAllKeys, count,
openCursor and openKeyCursor were commented out of the WebIDL. web.whatsapp.com
calls getAll and openCursor on an index while loading a logged-in session, got
"_.getAll is not a function", and hung on its loading screen forever.

The backend cannot answer these. Its schema has index_data and unique_index_data
tables, but nothing ever writes them: index entries are only ever deleted. Nor
could the backend compute them, since it stores values as opaque structured-clone
blobs and cannot evaluate a key path. So the records are derived in script, where
the values can be deserialized: read the object store, evaluate the index's key
path over each value, drop records the path does not apply to, expand multiEntry
arrays, sort into index order, and keep the first record per key for a unique
index. Correct, and linear in the size of the store; real index tables in the
backend are the destination, this is the road to it.

Cursors over an index reuse that: the records are synthesized on every step, not
just the first, because a cursor that forgot its index stopped after one record.

Fixes a bug in iterate_cursor along the way. Step 9.4 wrote the found record's
primary key straight to the cursor and never updated the local object store
position that step 11 later stores, so the running value was always stale: an
index cursor reported an undefined primaryKey and skipped every record that
shared an index key with its predecessor.

Verified at runtime over a four-record store with two indexes: getAll, getAllKeys
and count return the right rows, a multiEntry index matches on each array
element, and an index cursor walks Berlin/2, Cairo/4, Oslo/1, Oslo/3 in index
order with the right primary keys. With this, web.whatsapp.com finishes loading
and renders the chat list.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
